{
  "0": "Network architecture: apply LayerNorm to the GRU candidate-state preactivation, before tanh, while retaining existing hidden width, depth and gate equations. Normalizing candidate updates may stabilize recurrent dynamics across sparse histories without adding recurrent layers. Preserve embeddings, input features, scoring, history readout, loss and optimizer. Apply to all four datasets, with one trained model per category; update the predictor writer consistently.",
  "1": "Interaction mechanism: introduce a jointly trained diagonal bilinear compatibility, s(h,i)=sum_d w_d*h_d*e_i,d, initializing w to ones and retaining any existing score bias. This lets the model suppress unhelpful compatibility dimensions with few extra parameters and initially reproduces dot-product scoring. Preserve GRU topology, embeddings, features, readout, loss and optimizer. Apply to all four datasets with one checkpoint per category and matching predictor generation.",
  "2": "Feature construction: scale each existing input embedding by 1/sqrt(c_t), where c_t counts occurrences of that item in the user's causal prefix through position t. Compute counts from the supplied observed history only, excluding padding and prediction targets. This may reduce domination by repeated interactions while retaining their order. Keep learned embedding design, GRU topology, readout, scoring, loss and optimizer fixed. Apply to all four datasets with identical training and inference feature construction.",
  "3": "Sequence modeling: halve the current maximum input context length, rounding up, and retain only the most recent interactions in their original chronological order. Shorter context may reduce stale-interest interference and recurrent computation, particularly for noisy Electronics histories. Preserve supplied splits, training targets, feature definitions, embeddings, GRU topology, readout, scoring, loss and optimizer. Apply the same truncation at training and prediction across all four datasets, retaining one model per category."
}
